          "content": "### Mechanism: what physical or chemical change is being proposed?\n\nRemoving crystals as they form changes residual melt composition differently from keeping crystals in equilibrium with the liquid. To reason quantitatively about **fractional and equilibrium crystallization**, first distinguish the part of the system that is open to transport from the part treated as a closed parcel. Track changes in the mineral or melt assemblage, not just the name assigned to the whole rock. A rock records its latest preserved state together with some relics, so the working question is not simply \u201cWhat rock is this?\u201d but \u201cWhat sequence of processes could have produced these specific observations?\u201d\n\n### Evidence: what would a geologist actually measure?\n\nCrystal cumulates, incompatible-element enrichment and mineral zoning can constrain separation histories. Organize the field notebook as a sequence of observation, measurement conditions and tentative interpretation. Geological evidence can come from hand specimens, thin sections, mapped contacts, electron-microprobe analyses, isotopes or dated mineral domains. The methods are complementary rather than interchangeable. Do not describe a map-scale contact as a microscopic mineral reaction, or a crystal core analysis as a whole-rock average.\n\n### Competing interpretations and a discriminating test\n\nA trend in whole-rock silica alone does not uniquely establish fractional crystallization. The alternative is worth considering because two very different histories can converge on similar final textures or compositions. Use a simple Rayleigh trace-element model and test whether major-element balances are plausible. A test must be designed before the final preferred interpretation is chosen: otherwise the analyst risks using every possible feature as evidence for the same story. Identify the response variable, a comparison sample and the result each proposed process would predict.\n\nAn independent mineralogical check can be more valuable than simply increasing precision of an already biased major-element measurement. Plan discrimination first, then precision. \n\n### Worked quantitative example 11: Rayleigh residual melt concentration\n\n**Synthetic teaching inputs:** C0=2.1 ppm, f=0.38, bulk d=0.25 fixed; no replenishment. Treat the listed inputs as constructed exercise values, not measurements from a real locality. The mathematical relation in this intentionally simplified calculation is `2.1*(0.38)**(0.25-1)`. Substituting the stated values gives **4.3389 ppm**. Check whether the result has appropriate units and physical order of magnitude before using it.\n\n### Why this inference can fail\n\nDistribution coefficients depend on mineral assemblage and changing pressure\u2013temperature conditions. The scale of observation controls what the measured record can resolve. Two samples showing similar assemblages are not necessarily coeval; two mineral compositions agreeing within analytical precision are not necessarily in equilibrium. Evidence of replacement can survive after bulk chemistry has changed. An independent mineralogical check can be more valuable than simply increasing precision of an already biased major-element measurement. Plan discrimination first, then precision. No degree of numerical polishing eliminates a missing source reservoir or undocumented sample context.\n\n### Guided field or laboratory investigation\n\n1. Define the most diagnostic feature for **fractional and equilibrium crystallization** and record it without a causal verb.\n2. Sketch the predicted field or thin-section expression of the principal mechanism: removing crystals as they form changes residual melt composition differently from keeping crystals in equilibrium with the liquid.\n3. Describe an observation that would be expected under the rival: a trend in whole-rock silica alone does not uniquely establish fractional crystallization.\n4. Propose the sampling or measurement comparison: use a simple Rayleigh trace-element model and test whether major-element balances are plausible.\n5. Recompute the quantitative case after changing its largest uncertain input by a plausible amount; describe which conclusion survives.\n6. Produce a short evidence matrix that ranks the explanations as supported, consistent-but-not-unique, contradicted or untested.\n\n**Interpretive outcome:** Distinguish an illustrative trace-element curve from a calibrated magma evolution path. A strong answer states both the observation that supports a conclusion and the observation still needed to reject an alternative.\n\n### Review questions\n\n- Which measured variable provides the strongest test of **fractional and equilibrium crystallization**, and which confounder is most threatening?\n- Why might the competing hypothesis produce an apparently similar rock, and what data would discriminate?\n- Which parameter in the worked numerical example is least securely known in a real field setting?\n- Can a preserved texture be assigned to one instant in time, or might it be a record of overprinting?\n- What conclusion would you withdraw if a second laboratory contradicted the key measurement?",
